// THE POLYNOMIAL HASH MODULO THE MERSENNE PRIME p = 2^61 − 1, Rabin–Karp's
// and Carter–Wegman's: h ← (h·x + c) mod p. With h, x < p < 2^61 the product
// h·x is below 2^122, above the word and inside E = (−2^126, 2^126), and the
// only thing that reads it is the remainder by p, a constant: an intermediate
// the double word computes (ADR 0103), with every value of the program a word.
// Go, the JVM and JavaScript hold no double word, so the product is refused
// there, as a product above the word is (ADR 0019); under this harness's
// -checked it is a trap, which fires at the fourth input on Go and the JVM, and
// on JavaScript the remainder of a pair by p is not built. Each skip says which.
//
// inputs: 0 1 2 100 100000
// expect: 0 0 1 371942649230435435 695070068227922815
// skip: go because "panic: int overflow"
// skip: js because "not a power of two: not built (ir/pairs.go)"
// skip: java because "long overflow"
fn run(n) {
    loop i = 0, h = 0 { i >= n -> h; else -> again(i + 1, (h * 1234567890123456789 + i % 251) % (2^61 - 1)) }
}
